 4 files changed, 30 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/library/market-data/futures-fixings.md b/docs/library/market-data/futures-fixings.md
index cefb156..cb87e90 100644
--- a/docs/library/market-data/futures-fixings.md
+++ b/docs/library/market-data/futures-fixings.md
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ Quoted from the declaration in `src/goosedove/excel/market_data.py`, which `test
 
 ## The library checks
 
-Five rules, in this order, the first failure being the message. The first is the index's kind: the days are checked against a holiday calendar, and only a futures index has one, so an IFERC or a Gas Daily index is refused naming its kind, as the calendar names kinds. The second comes with the columns: the settle column has one row per date, since the seam never compares two columns and a series is rows. The third is the curve's, which the owner stated as no duplicate dates and the dates ascending: each date is after the row above, and that one comparison covers both, keeping one fixing per day in the order a reader finds a day by. The fourth and fifth are the ticket's of #79, that creation detects a fixing on a day the market was closed and a business day without a fixing: each date is a business day, Monday to Friday and not a holiday, at either end of the series as in the middle; and no business day between the first date and the last is without a fixing. With every date a business day and the dates increasing, the series is whole exactly when each date is the business day after the one above, which is one `np.busday_offset` over the column and one comparison, as [time.md](../time.md) decided business days would be counted: numpy's functions over the calendar's list, a week of Monday to Friday, and no loop. Rows count as the sheet counts them, the first row of the column being row 1, and the message names the column by its argument name.
+Five rules, in this order, the first failure being the message. The first is the index's kind: the days are checked against a holiday calendar, and only a futures index has one, so an IFERC or a Gas Daily index is refused naming its kind, as the calendar names kinds. The second comes with the columns: the settle column has one row per date, since the seam never compares two columns and a series is rows. The third is the curve's, which the owner stated as no duplicate dates and the dates ascending: each date is after the row above, and that one comparison covers both, keeping one fixing per day in the order a reader finds a day by. The fourth and fifth are the ticket's of #79, that creation detects a fixing on a day the market was closed and a business day without a fixing: each date is a business day, Monday to Friday and not a holiday, at either end of the series as in the middle; and no business day between the first date and the last is without a fixing. With every date a business day and the dates increasing, the series is whole exactly when each date is the business day after the one above, which is exactly when the business days from the first date up to the last number one fewer than the rows: one `np.busday_count` of two dates, and only when that count is short one `np.busday_offset` over the column and one comparison to name the first business day without a fixing, as [time.md](../time.md) decided business days would be counted: numpy's functions over the calendar's list, a week of Monday to Friday, and no loop. Rows count as the sheet counts them, the first row of the column being row 1, and the message names the column by its argument name.
 
 | Rule | Message |
 |---|---|
diff --git a/src/goosedove/market_data/futures_fixings.py b/src/goosedove/market_data/futures_fixings.py
index 207624c..557c338 100644
--- a/src/goosedove/market_data/futures_fixings.py
+++ b/src/goosedove/market_data/futures_fixings.py
@@ -44,14 +44,15 @@ def futures_fixings(
     weekend day or a holiday in the series is refused, at either end as in the middle. And no
     business day between the first date and the last is without a fixing, since a gap is a row
     the desk did not paste: with every date a business day and the dates increasing, the series
-    is whole exactly when each date is the business day after the one above, one numpy call
-    over the column. The first and last dates bound the check, so nothing is asked for before
-    the first or after the last, and a holiday outside them changes nothing. The holidays are
-    read as numpy reads them: a day listed twice counts once, and a day listed on a weekend adds
-    nothing. Both columns are copied and then frozen, so the fixings own their data: a Python
-    caller keeps whatever it passed, and a later change to an input never changes a fixing; the
-    index is kept by reference, the one the desk paired the series with. Rows count as the sheet
-    counts them, the first row of the column being row 1.
+    is whole exactly when the business days from the first date up to the last number one fewer
+    than the rows, one numpy count of two dates, and the business day after each date is found
+    only to name the first one missing. The first and last dates bound the check, so nothing is
+    asked for before the first or after the last, and a holiday outside them changes nothing.
+    The holidays are read as numpy reads them: a day listed twice counts once, and a day listed
+    on a weekend adds nothing. Both columns are copied and then frozen, so the fixings own their
+    data: a Python caller keeps whatever it passed, and a later change to an input never
+    changes a fixing; the index is kept by reference, the one the desk paired the series with.
+    Rows count as the sheet counts them, the first row of the column being row 1.
     """
     if index.kind is not Kind.FUTURES:
         raise ValueError(f"index is {described(index.kind, 'index')}, expected a futures index")
@@ -73,9 +74,13 @@ def futures_fixings(
         weekday = WEEKDAYS[date.fromisoformat(str(days[k])).weekday()]
         reason = weekday if weekday in ("Saturday", "Sunday") else "holiday"
         raise ValueError(f"date row {k + 1} is {days[k]}, a {reason}, expected a business day")
-    following = np.busday_offset(days[:-1], 1, busdaycal=calendar)
-    skipped = following != days[1:]
-    if skipped.any():
+    # Every date a business day and the dates increasing, the rows but the last are that many
+    # business days from the first date up to the last, so the series is whole exactly when
+    # those business days number one fewer than the rows: one np.busday_count of two dates. The
+    # business day after each date is made only when one is missing, to name the first.
+    if days.size > 1 and np.busday_count(days[0], days[-1], busdaycal=calendar) != days.size - 1:
+        following = np.busday_offset(days[:-1], 1, busdaycal=calendar)
+        skipped = following != days[1:]
         k = int(skipped.argmax())  # following[k] is the first business day without a fixing
         raise ValueError(
             f"date row {k + 2} is {days[k + 1]}, expected {following[k]}, "
